1
00:00:03,990 --> 00:00:07,698
Which metrics should an Amazon seller watch?

2
00:00:07,710 --> 00:00:09,380
I would highlight at least three.

3
00:00:09,630 --> 00:00:12,200
First, contribution margin per unit.

4
00:00:12,390 --> 00:00:14,900
It is more than price minus purchase cost.

5
00:00:14,970 --> 00:00:17,298
Consider all the relevant variable costs

6
00:00:17,310 --> 00:00:19,520
that arise from selling each unit.

7
00:00:19,890 --> 00:00:21,498
Second, advertising efficiency.

8
00:00:21,510 --> 00:00:23,780
Two metrics are easily confused.

9
00:00:23,850 --> 00:00:28,640
ACOS is ad spend divided by ad-attributed sales.

10
00:00:29,190 --> 00:00:33,680
TACOS is ad spend divided by total store revenue.

11
00:00:34,110 --> 00:00:35,478
In our scenario

12
00:00:35,490 --> 00:00:37,218
ad spend is $20,000.

13
00:00:37,230 --> 00:00:39,020
Total revenue is $100,000.

14
00:00:39,210 --> 00:00:42,200
So TACOS is 20%.

15
00:00:42,750 --> 00:00:46,098
Without the sales attributed to ads

16
00:00:46,110 --> 00:00:48,170
we cannot calculate ACOS.

17
00:00:48,690 --> 00:00:51,680
Third, inventory turnover and cash flow.

18
00:00:52,050 --> 00:00:54,498
Unsold goods tie up cash

19
00:00:54,510 --> 00:00:56,960
and can keep generating storage costs.

20
00:00:57,150 --> 00:00:59,180
What really matters

21
00:00:59,610 --> 00:01:01,880
is not just sales growth

22
00:01:02,310 --> 00:01:04,218
but how much remains

23
00:01:04,230 --> 00:01:06,500
after all the relevant costs.
